High-level steps
---------------------------------------------
Prepare repository

Add a build script named build.sh at repo root that creates output.txt with "Hello World".
Commit & push to GitHub.
Expose Jenkins to GitHub (if Jenkins not publicly reachable)

Use HTTPS on a public URL or use ngrok for local testing: ngrok http 8080
Note the public URL (or your real Jenkins domain) for webhook setup: https://<your-jenkins-host>/github-webhook/

------------------------------------------------
Create Freestyle job in Jenkins

Jenkins > New Item > Freestyle project > Name: First-Freestyle-Job
Source Code Management > Git > Repository URL: https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>.git
Credentials: Add “Username with password” or “Secret text”/PAT. If using HTTPS, prefer a PAT (personal access token). For cloning private repos use credentials; for public repo none required.
Build Triggers > Check: "GitHub hook trigger for GITScm polling"
Optionally also check "Poll SCM" and set Schedule to H/5 * * * * for every 5 minutes (not recommended in prod).
Build > Add build step > Execute shell: ./build.sh
Post-build Actions > Archive the artifacts: output.txt
Save.

------------------------------------
Configure GitHub webhook

GitHub repo > Settings > Webhooks > Add webhook
Payload URL: https://<your-jenkins-host>/github-webhook/
Content type: application/json
Secret: optional (if set, configure the same in Jenkins GitHub plugin / global credentials)
Which events: choose "Just the push event" (or "Send me everything")
Add webhook. Use "Recent Deliveries" to view payloads.
Test
-------------------------------------------
Push a commit to the branch Jenkins is configured to build (default: master/main).
In Jenkins: Job > Build History should show a build triggered by GitHub push. Open Console Output.
Job > Workspace should show repository files (or check the build's workspace link).
-------------------------------------------------
Troubleshooting checklist

If webhook shows delivery error: confirm Jenkins URL is publicly reachable and not blocked by firewall; validate SSL if HTTPS.
If cloning fails: check credentials and PAT scopes (repo read access for private repos; repo:status not usually necessary for basic clone).
If job not triggered: verify "GitHub plugin" is installed and GitHub webhook triggers enabled in job.
Use Jenkins system logs (/var/log/jenkins/jenkins.log) for deeper errors.
For local testing, use ngrok and ensure the ngrok URL is the webhook target.
----------------------------------------------------
Suggested files

A simple build.sh that writes "Hello World" to output.txt (make executable)

(If you already have lab1.sh and prefer to use it, you can either rename it to build.sh or point the job's Execute shell to ./lab1.sh. Your lab1.sh writes "Cloned the repository" to lab1.txt twice — if that's intended, no change required.)

Optional: sample Jenkins Freestyle job config.xml (you can use this to create a job by placing it in JENKINS_HOME/jobs/First-Freestyle-Job/config.xml and reloading Jenkins config, or use the UI). Edit repository URL and credentialsId before use.
-----------------------------------------------------

Useful commands to simulate/test webhooks

Trigger Jenkins GitHub webhook (simulate GitHub POST) — replace URLs and tokens as needed: curl -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" --data '{"ref":"refs/heads/main","repository": {"full_name":"<owner>/<repo>"}}' https://<your-jenkins-host>/github-webhook/

Test clone from Jenkins server (on Jenkins host) to validate credentials: git clone https://<username>:<PAT>@github.com/<owner>/<repo>.git /tmp/test-clone
---------------------------------------------------
PAT scopes and credential notes

For cloning private repos: repo (or at least repo:read) scopes on PAT.
For managing webhooks via API: admin:repo_hook (not required for simple webhook delivery).
For public repos no PAT required to clone, but recommended for reliable behavior.
